{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FQKBhyMEu5Nucz8JjP6pE5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Conclusion}\\label{sec:conclusion}\n2\t\n3\tStandard confusion-matrix correction---the default in PPI, DSL, and CSS practice---can reverse causal conclusions when misclassification rates vary across subgroups.\n4\tWe derived an exact bias identity (Theorem~\\ref{thm:bias_identity}) explaining when and why, verified across synthetic grids, 11 LLM--dataset combinations, and demographic subgroups.\n5\tThe identity yields a diagnostic (Proposition~\\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts}) that flags harmful correction from error rates alone, and prescribes per-subgroup correction that eliminates sign reversal, given sufficient error heterogeneity and expert budget."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017qp4rUWunC8kWKX2aGEtMU","session_id":"44ebc33a-5f0e-4594-9646-2fa2afa29759","uuid":"1d5c78cd-9cc5-48cb-9c8e-a25200bc9fce","timestamp":"2026-05-23T20:28:58.068Z"}
